<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Time of Troubles</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1598">1598</a>–<a href="/years/1613">1613</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Time of Troubles was a period of political crisis, famine, civil war and foreign intervention in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> between the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rurik-dynasty">Rurik dynasty</a> in 1598 and the election of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-of-russia">Michael Romanov</a> as tsar in 1613. Pretenders claiming to be <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iv-of-russia">Ivan IV</a>'s dead son Dmitry won wide support, and Polish-Lithuanian forces occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> from 1610 to 1612, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> seized Novgorod.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/time-of-troubles.jpg" alt="Time of Troubles" width="320" height="376" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Konstantin Makovsky · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Appeal_of_Minin.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Tsar Feodor I, a pious and weak ruler who took little interest in politics, governed through his brother-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boris-godunov">Boris Godunov</a>. His younger half-brother Dmitry died in May 1591; an investigative commission concluded that the boy had accidentally cut his own throat during an epileptic seizure. When Feodor died in January 1598 without an heir, the main line of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rurik-dynasty">Rurik dynasty</a> ended. Godunov was crowned in September 1598 after a sham Zemsky Sobor had elected him. The famine of 1601 to 1603, possibly caused by the eruption of the volcano Huaynaputina in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, ruined harvests, killed a large share of the population and damaged Godunov's reputation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In 1603 a man claiming to be Dmitry appeared in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a>, and in October 1604 he crossed into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. After Godunov died in 1605, False Dmitry I entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> and was crowned on 21 July. He was killed in a Moscow uprising on 17 May 1606, and Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasili-iv-of-russia">Vasily Shuisky</a> was proclaimed tsar. A second pretender, False Dmitry II, appeared in 1607 and besieged Moscow from Tushino. In 1609 King Sigismund III of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smolensk">Smolensk</a>, and at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-klushino">Battle of Klushino</a> in 1610 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> routed Shuisky's army.</p><p>Shuisky was forced to become a monk in July 1610, and a council of Seven Boyars invited Sigismund's son Wladyslaw to rule. Polish troops occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> occupied Novgorod in 1611. Polish and German troops put down a Moscow uprising in March 1611, killing about 7,000. A second national militia, raised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuzma-minin">Kuzma Minin</a> and led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dmitry-pozharsky">Prince Dmitry Pozharsky</a>, reached Moscow in July 1612, and on 27 October 1612 the Polish garrison surrendered.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The Zemsky Sobor elected the sixteen-year-old Mikhail Romanov tsar on 21 February 1613, and he was crowned on 21 July. The Romanov dynasty ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/february-revolution">February Revolution</a> of 1917. The historian Chester Dunning calls it a tragic irony that Mikhail ended the Troubles partly by crushing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossacks</a> who had brought him to power. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ingrian-war">Ingrian War</a> ended with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-stolbovo">Treaty of Stolbovo</a> in 1617, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/truce-of-deulino">Truce of Deulino</a> of 1619 ended fighting with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. Russia kept its independence but made substantial territorial concessions, ceding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ingria">Ingria</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, though most of the losses were recovered in the following century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Time of Troubles” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>During the <b>Time of Troubles</b> Polish forces occupied Moscow from 1610 until a national militia led by Kuzma Minin and Prince Dmitry Pozharsky expelled them in 1612, and the peace treaties of Stolbovo (1617) and Deulino (1618) cost Russia its Baltic coast and Smolensk.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tsardom-of-russia">Tsardom of Russia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>An assembly of the land chose sixteen-year-old Mikhail Romanov as tsar (21 February Old Style), ending the <b>Time of Troubles</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-21">21 February in history</a></li></ul></section>
